 "reviewer": "astra (gpt-6-astra ultra) + grok (grok-4.7)",
 "report": "`--limit 0` now returns 0, and leaving out `--limit` still gives 10. The fix is merged into `main` as PR #1 (https://github.com/fysoul17/rpd8-20261019a-m08/pull/1). I sent it as a PR and merged it because your project instructions say to deliver that way by default.\n\n**Cause:** `parse_limit` ended with `return args.limit or 10`. Python treats 0 as false, so an explicit `--limit 0` was replaced by the default.\n\n**What I changed** (only the two files the request allowed):\n- `src/limitcli/__init__.py`: `--limit` now uses argparse's own default (`default=10`) and the function returns `args.limit` as is. The `is not None` guard is gone, since the value can no longer be `None`. Negative values are still rejected with `parser.error`.\n- `tests/test_regression.py` (new): checks that\n  - `--limit 0` and `--limit=0` both return 0;\n  - leaving `--limit` out returns 10;\n  - positive limits come back unchanged;\n  - all of these still exit with code 2: `-1`, `bad`, `1.5`, `--limit` with no value, and an unknown argument.\n\n**What I verified:**\n- With the old code, the new zero test fails (1 failed, 13 passed). With the fix, all 14 pass under `python3 -B -m pytest -q`.\n- I ran the same check again in the separate delivery worktree before committing, and once more on `main` after the merge: 14 passed.\n- `docs/user-notes.txt`, the existing tests and `caller.json` are unchanged. I didn't change Git config, dependencies or installed tools.\n- After the merge, I removed my uncommitted copies of the edits from your checkout and fast-forwarded `main` to the merge commit, so the working tree is clean. The temporary delivery worktree was removed automatically.\n\n**Unresolved:** nothing.",
